# goal Make a running turn legible without changing what the turn returns

An ACP agent says what it is doing — `tool_call`, `tool_call_update`, `plan` — and a
chat completion has no field for any of it. This renders those updates as short
notes so a human watching a channel can see a twenty-minute turn progressing instead
of a typing indicator.

# invariant Notes are never part of the answer {#never_content}

They travel in `reasoning_content`, alongside the thinking already there, and never
in `text`. A trace written into the answer BECOMES the answer: "running bash" ends
up in what the caller quotes, stores, and replies to, and on the next turn it comes
back as an assistant message the agent has to read as its own words.

This is the same reasoning that keeps `tool_call` events out of the text in
[agent.js.hint#tool_calls_are_progress](agent.js.hint) — rendering them changes
where they go, not what they are.

# rule Report transitions, not states {#transitions}

ACP repeats itself. `tool_call_update` fires for every status change and usually
carries the whole call again; `plan_update` fires per entry transition and most of
those leave the summary identical. A renderer without memory narrates the same work
three or four times, which is worse than silence — the reader stops trusting the
column.

So `Progress` remembers: a tool call is worth a note when it is first SEEN and when
it FAILS, a plan when its summary line actually changes. Success gets no note; the
next line implies it.

**One instance per turn.** Sharing one across turns is the failure that looks like a
feature working: the second turn goes quiet about anything the first mentioned.

`tool_call` and `tool_call_update` are deliberately handled by the same path.
Agents do not agree on which one opens a call — some send an update first, and the
bundled fixture's initial `tool_call` already carries a terminal status — so first
sighting is the only reliable start signal.

# rule A diff is measured, never pasted {#diff_shape}

`ToolCallContent` of type `diff` carries `oldText` and `newText` in full, and a
legitimate edit can be a whole-file rewrite. Pasting that into a progress note
buries every other line of the turn in a chat window.

`diffShape` counts instead: strip the common head and tail, report `+added/-removed`
against the path. It is a line count and not a real diff algorithm, which is enough
to say how big an edit was and costs nothing. `oldText: null` means a new file, so
every line counts as an addition.

# rule A tool call is many updates, and the useful parts are in different ones {#accumulate}

Captured from a live `claude-agent-acp` on 2026-08-12: ONE Bash command arrives as
six updates.

| # | carries |
| --- | --- |
| 1 `tool_call` | `title: "Terminal"`, `rawInput: {}` — the command is still being streamed |
| 2 `tool_call_update` | the real title and `rawInput.command`. **No status.** |
| 3 `tool_call_update` | the same, plus a description |
| 4 `tool_call_update` | `_meta.claudeCode.toolResponse` (stdout/stderr) |
| 5 `tool_call_update` | `_meta.terminal_output.data`. **No status, no title.** |
| 6 `tool_call_update` | `status: "completed"` and `_meta.terminal_exit.exit_code`. **No output, no title.** |

Three consequences, and the first version of this file got all three wrong:

- **Name it by the LAST title, not the first.** Taking the first names every
  command "Terminal", which is the adapter's placeholder for one it has not
  finished reading.
- **Announce when the call knows what it is doing**, i.e. once `rawInput` is
  non-empty — or when it reaches a terminal status, so nothing stays unannounced
  forever. Announcing on first sighting prints the placeholder.
- **Read the output and the exit code from ACCUMULATED state.** They arrive in
  updates 5 and 6; reading either from the update that carries the status finds
  nothing, which is why every command first looked successful and silent.

# rule A command's output is tailed, in whichever shape it arrives {#terminal_output}

For an `execute` call the output usually IS the interesting part, and agents send
it two different ways depending on a capability the CLIENT declared. `terminalResult`
reads both, so neither the flag nor its absence changes what a reader sees:

- `_meta.terminal_output.data` with `_meta.terminal_exit.exit_code` — sent by
  `claude-agent-acp` and `codex-acp` when `clientCapabilities._meta.terminal_output`
  is advertised. The only shape that carries an exit code.
- a ```` ```console ```` fenced block in the ordinary `content` — the same agents'
  fallback otherwise.

Only the LAST `progressOutputLines` lines are shown, because that is where a
command says what happened: a build prints a thousand lines of progress and one
line of verdict. The bound is not politeness — this lands in a chat post, and an
unbounded `npm test` buries every other line of the turn.

A zero exit code is not a failure. Reporting `exit 0` as `✗` would train the
reader to ignore the mark that matters.

# rule Every note is one line {#one_line}

`oneLine` collapses whitespace and clips, because these are read in a column next to
everything else the reader is doing. A tool title containing a newline would
otherwise break the shape of the whole trace.

# test progress

- a tool call is noted once, however many updates repeat it
- a failed tool call is noted; a completed one is not
- a diff becomes `± path +N/-M`, and a new file counts every line as added
- an unchanged plan summary produces nothing on the second update
- an update kind this module does not render returns null rather than throwing
